> > So, just wondering why did you remove specific types of polearms?
>
> Because in Nethack right now, each weapon is defined almost entirely by
> a single factor: its damage.  This renders the majority of polearms
> junk, and indeed the majority of _weapons_ junk in comparison to the
> Holy Longsword, Katana, Spetum, and Halberd.  In fact, weapon choice is
> based not at all on flavor, but on raw average damage -- look at the
> spoilers for a good example.  They might as well have "Use this one"
> painted on the appropriate lines.  That's no good.
>
> Also, part of the reason I removed specific types of polearms is that
> there's a level of detail in any given game that actually _detracts_ from
> the user's enjoyment of it, and for *Hack, polearms are it.  It's one thing
> to have flavor via multiple types of weapons; it's another thing when
> there are so many "angled poleaxe", "long poleaxe", "sharp stick"
> unidentified weapons that they might as well be dungeon litter.
>
> Not all objects have to be useful, but to have such a large collection
> of use_less_ objects is rather annoying.  Soldiers and trolls can still
> be specifically generated with a particular one, so they aren't
> _completely_ gone yet; but eventually a lot more things are going to
> change about weaponry and how it's done.  This is just the front end.
>
> Basically, there should be a reason to use any particular weapon
> _besides_ "can I get Basic in it" and "does it do max avg damage?"
>
I just finished a Lawful Spork knight and it gave me an idea for a
possible weapon-system change, though it might be too extreme.
Instead of having the comparatively feeble artifact alignment blast
associated with wielding a cross-aligned weapon (and I'm thinking of
my lawful knight wielding Lifestealer here, in particular), how about
instead have an alignment penalty or artifact-blast for each _use_ of
the artifact.  In the case of a weapon, a use is considered an
attack.  This is similar to the penalty for Lawfuls each time they
fire poisoned projectiles.

Speaking of which, does the use of Dirge by a Lawful character carry
an alignment penalty?

